+    iteration before being read, so its value at a [continue] is dead: the next
+    iteration never reads it, and the joins at the loop entry discard it (they
+    join it with [⊥]). When such a value holds shared borrows (and no loans or
+    mutable borrows, so that discarding it gives nothing back), discard it
+    before the joins instead, and end the borrows and abstractions which were
+    only kept alive by it, e.g. the shared borrow returned by an indexing
+    ([let r = &lim[0]; ... *r ...]) and the abstractions of that call.
+    Otherwise, the join has to reconcile the borrow graph of these
+    abstractions, which exists in the continue context only. *)
